-\enabstract{Dynamic inflow during high-maneuverability flight can cause a mismatch in the static thrust mapping, resulting in the actual thrust of the rotor deviating from the commanded value. To improve thrust prediction and disturbance suppression capabilities at the actuator level, this paper establishes a mechanistic model that maps the motion state of a multirotor UAV to the axial thrust of a single rotor, and calculates thrust by combining measured blade geometry, airfoil polar curves, and a non-uniform induced inflow element model. Based on this, a closed-loop control method is proposed that uses a rotor inverse model as feedforward and direct thrust measurements as feedback. A rotating test bench with adjustable blade disk angle of attack and turntable angular velocity was constructed. The results show that the developed model can accurately describe the thrust variation patterns within the test envelope, and the composite control of model feedforward and direct force feedback can effectively suppress strong on-flow disturbances on the single-rotor test bench.}
+\enabstract{Dynamic inflow during aggressive flight can invalidate static thrust mappings and cause rotor thrust to deviate from its command. To improve thrust prediction and disturbance suppression at the actuator level, this paper develops a mechanistic model that maps multirotor motion states to the axial thrust of a single rotor. Thrust is calculated using measured blade geometry, airfoil polars, and blade element theory with nonuniform induced inflow. A closed-loop controller combines rotor inverse-model feedforward with direct thrust feedback. A rotating test bench with adjustable rotor disk angle of attack and freestream speed is constructed to evaluate model predictions and control performance. The model captures thrust variations within the tested envelope. In actuator-level single-rotor tests under strong axial inflow, combined feedforward and feedback reduces normalized RMSE by 45.7\%--74.3\% relative to feedback alone. Control performance under oblique inflow and benefits for full-vehicle flight control remain to be validated.}
 
 \enkeyword{Multirotor unmanned aerial vehicle, High-maneuverability flight, Thrust feedback control, Blade element momentum theory}
     
